{"key":"att_ger_onager","land_unit":"att_ger_onager","name":"Onager","culture":"german","campaign":"main","category":"Siege Engine","class":"Siege Artillery","caste":"Light","group":"Artillery","card":"att_ger_onager","sources":["recruit"],"men":100,"mounts":0,"cost":800,"upkeep":30,"turns":2,"campaign_cap":3,"stats":{"melee_attack":14,"melee_defence":14,"shield_defence":14,"charge_bonus":8,"weapon_damage":15,"weapon_ap_damage":14,"bonus_v_infantry":0,"bonus_v_cavalry":20,"armour":2,"shield_armour":17,"missile_block":40,"morale":30,"hit_points":82,"mount_hit_points":0,"speed":4.1,"accuracy":0},"abilities":[{"name":"Barrage","text":"Loose projectiles more frequently for an extended period of time."}],"attributes":[{"name":"Cannot Run","text":"This unit cannot run and will only move at walking pace."}],"short":"The most powerful throwing machines in the Late Roman period were the ballista and the onager (Veg., IV, 29). Vegetius wrote that the weight of stones thrown by the onager was proportional to the thickness and size of its ropes (Veg., IV, 22). Onagers were capable of destroying not only the enemy's troops, but also their war machines and buildings (Veg., IV, 22). In addition to stone bombs, pots with ignition mixture could be used as projectiles in the 4th century.","history":"One of the simplest forms of one-armed catapult, the onager relied on twisted skeins of animal sinew which provided the power to hurl either rocks or shot. Inaccurate but powerful, it was an effective siege machine against buildings and fortifications, but was of far less use on the battlefield unless firing into massed infantry ranks. First mentioned by Philon of Byzantium in his treatise on ‘Mechanics’, the onager made use of a sling-like cradle attached to a firing arm. This arm was inserted, twisted, and propelled rocks or shot through torsion. As the firing arm was released, the sling-like cradle would unhook, firing the projectile. A padded beam was incorporated into the housing frame to restrain the arm. Larger machines could require as many as eight or ten crewmen, and the incredible recoil meant that they could not be easily or safely mounted on walls or towers. Quickly constructed wherever wood was readily available, onagers soon became a staple of ancient siege warfare.","strengths":["Very low damage and armour penetration","Very weak in melee","Very poor morale"],"factions":[{"key":"aorsi","name":"Aorsians"},{"key":"att_fact_alamanni","name":"Alamans"},{"key":"att_fact_alani","name":"Alans"},{"key":"att_fact_angli","name":"Angles"},{"key":"att_fact_antes","name":"Anteans"},{"key":"att_fact_bastarnae","name":"Bastarnians"},{"key":"att_fact_burgundii","name":"Burgundians"},{"key":"att_fact_ebdani","name":"Ebdanians"},{"key":"att_fact_franci","name":"Franks"},{"key":"att_fact_gepidae","name":"Gepids"},{"key":"att_fact_greuthingi","name":"Greuthungians"},{"key":"att_fact_iazyges","name":"Iazyges"},{"key":"att_fact_iuti","name":"Jutes"},{"key":"att_fact_langobardi","name":"Langobards"},{"key":"att_fact_lugii","name":"Scirians"},{"key":"att_fact_marcomanni","name":"Marcomans"},{"key":"att_fact_ostrogothi","name":"Ostrogoths"},{"key":"att_fact_picti","name":"Picts"},{"key":"att_fact_quadi","name":"Quadians"},{"key":"att_fact_roxolani","name":"Roxolanians"},{"key":"att_fact_rugii","name":"Rugians"},{"key":"att_fact_sclaveni","name":"Sclavenians"},{"key":"att_fact_separatist_alamanni","name":"Alamannic Separatists"},{"key":"att_fact_separatist_alanic","name":"Alanic Separatists"},{"key":"att_fact_separatist_antes","name":"Antean Separatists"},{"key":"att_fact_separatist_burgundii","name":"Burgundian Separatists"},{"key":"att_fact_separatist_celtic","name":"Celtic Separatists"},{"key":"att_fact_separatist_dani","name":"Danish Separatists"},{"key":"att_fact_separatist_ebdani","name":"Ebdanian Separatists"},{"key":"att_fact_separatist_frankish","name":"Frankish Separatists"},{"key":"att_fact_separatist_germanic_east","name":"East Germanic Separatist